#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20190404-fcaa-57f2-aafa-897dab3e7c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20190404-fcaa-57f2-aafa-897dab3e7c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247693} :answer-field/id #uuid "57644421-8dba-531a-b578-d218cbf56c15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247692} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "390024c3-52ec-5c79-8275-4b218bd01281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "390024c3-52ec-5c79-8275-4b218bd01281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247695} :answer-field/id #uuid "f61050a1-81bd-58bb-95c1-89560f62a7b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b4d713f-1d80-5450-80b8-4fa07a5cf04b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-322578" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/84ba52159416b3b420daa1ebd82dccac2411aee420201b9b9604937995d9553c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=s,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=t,$ and $L$ divides $\\overset{―}{ST}$ in the ratio $SL:LT=11:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OL}$ via the vectors $s$ and $t.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OL}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$s+$\n{{field-2}}\n$t$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OL}=\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=s,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{SL}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OST,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =\\overset{\\to }{SO}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-s+t \\\\  & =t-s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $SL:LT=11:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SL}=\\frac{11}{15}\\overset{\\to }{ST}=\\frac{11}{15}(t-s).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OL} & =\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL} \\\\  & =s+\\frac{11}{15}(t-s) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}s+\\frac{11}{15}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95c5e7b7-a0d5-5049-a2c6-541e2a0005e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247698} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfcf2c8-f723-5daf-8dc5-06dcc5431fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247699} {:answer/id #uuid "d241a433-7b03-5e75-aff5-a32c258a3c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247700} {:answer/id #uuid "ab54c472-ef01-5650-8986-1c41aea026d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247701} {:answer/id #uuid "ba914d18-2de4-53c6-be4f-3dc62429ba06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab54c472-ef01-5650-8986-1c41aea026d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247701} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a73acf5-2ea9-5abf-9989-147935bc2ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bcf8e68-866e-5087-9911-7b4d6947a1cb" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-367051" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/0f49938e84521acbfe0e44190190ae8b4fe58687a3cc241dbe9fc4a3480e37e8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+4b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/6af6aff8a4aa111e6eb74f8046a4ec01582c1cee7fcf922e443d17d0348da7f4.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =4b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+4b=-3a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d3b3f27-4711-58a5-b24b-deba3c579cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247705} {:answer/id #uuid "d006b6ef-75e1-57ee-8d1d-8eff2c1e60b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247706} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb04ecd-838d-50f4-8fef-61cb27ee8201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247707} {:answer/id #uuid "dff7240e-ce56-5859-a1d3-d8ca1e9305bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =0$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247708} {:answer/id #uuid "56d024e4-4021-595c-a6fa-3248f67ec539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d006b6ef-75e1-57ee-8d1d-8eff2c1e60b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247706} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d41908-1551-5127-b081-670540142a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247704}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61ebce66-7134-5f7b-9efc-9623368f4d93" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-42657" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b29adb9-e20d-5fd9-a0f3-a8db9dccdb55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ = 3.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095318} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfaefca-cb49-5b5b-b9e7-c3754b7e2a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095319} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e96c47-f79e-536a-ad1d-9d6336897d1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ =-2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095320} {:answer/id #uuid "eb745163-55b7-5ba8-8112-cdfc6e777bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095321} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed03bd2-2df5-5a7a-92ac-9d5eae1675b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed03bd2-2df5-5a7a-92ac-9d5eae1675b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095322} :answer-field/id #uuid "eba78439-ab78-53b0-9a0c-a91774dec622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6df187f-d344-54eb-95de-18be02ffa7f9" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-42670" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/q-42670.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t = λr + μs$, find $λ$ and $μ$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/a0d9fa1c366945e5e789b6361f929357af163db2e9be4a3ccc9023c12486aca6.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=2|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-2r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-2r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-2r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdd61204-8f09-5296-af14-92ca1f4c1559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247712} {:answer/id #uuid "e60395f9-6e6c-5b57-b887-237d31d14eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247713} {:answer/id #uuid "c279baf2-75e5-56c6-bf1e-2d7dd75dbb8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247714} {:answer/id #uuid "680fd7b7-903b-5628-acee-36dd0c0b786c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247715} {:answer/id #uuid "06e0ad98-d22f-54d5-a643-f26e6f82c2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680fd7b7-903b-5628-acee-36dd0c0b786c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247715} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e5c7a5f-72cb-541b-bbbc-a366ca28e59d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247711}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc8ad121-7bdb-532a-9ac2-df30cc8779fc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-77451" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/28faea3a553c505a8a5ab9bc7dd6e784dd23a47c336c70d31218fbe249d57cff.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/52421034c2919620a0a9aae844db0eba2d396e5d6f1152d69bee40ff56c4d8ad.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=-3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}]}
